WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.110
I wish that I could say
let's put 2020 behind us

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.620
and never think about it again.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.280
But I'm afraid we have to look back

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.830
at what was for many
a very exhausting year

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:12.883
and do something only slightly
less exhausting, our taxes.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.010
2020 brings with it all kinds
of special considerations

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.270
when it comes to your income tax filing.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.000
Stimulus payments, did
you receive unemployment,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.330
working from home?

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.300
Let's put on some chill new age music

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.350
and see if we can demystify some of this.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.200
So let's say somebody
lost their job last year

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.110
and they received those
enhanced unemployment benefits.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.325
What does that mean for their tax filing?

00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:48.340
Very important, unemployment is taxable

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.920
and they have to make
sure that it's included

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.800
on this 2020 return.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.700
They are not gonna get
the form directly mailed,

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.850
it appears to them.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.800
They're gonna have to go
online and get the 1099-G form

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.630
that will tell them how much is taxable

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:07.909
and if they paid any
taxes in advance on them.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:10.213
What about the stimulus payments
people have been getting?

00:01:10.213 --> 00:01:13.460
The stimulus payments are not taxable.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.320
So you do not have to
worry about reporting it

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.960
when you get to your return.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.190
Some people didn't get
benefits of a stimulus payment

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:22.200
who deserved it.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.760
If your income was higher
in 2019 taxes that they used

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.450
or 2018, you may have
not gotten the stimulus

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.110
because your income was too high.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.867
But lo and behold, 2020
comes and you made less money

00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:37.690
and you now qualify.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:41.300
So you can now apply for
the stimulus payments

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.680
when you file your 2020 taxes
and get what you didn't get

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.050
because your income was too high

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.430
when they based it on the prior year.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.310
What is the home office deduction

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.463
and how can I take advantage of it?

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.190
That's been a question that
many people are asking.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:58.470
If you have your own business,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.270
yes, the home office deduction
is yours for the taking.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.540
If there's a room you use for business

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.810
and you don't have an office that you use.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.360
But the bad news is a few years ago,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.210
they changed the deduction for people

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.320
who are on W2 for employees.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.320
So an employee cannot take
any employee business expenses

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.557
as a deduction, including
the home office deduction.

00:02:23.557 --> 00:02:25.570
So let's say that I am that freelancer.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:29.730
There are certain items
that I'm buying specifically

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.740
for work that I want to deduct at 100%.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:36.333
Can you tell me if they
are legit deductions?

00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:37.166
Go for it.

00:02:37.166 --> 00:02:38.660
Okay, office plant.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.377
I'm gonna give you that one.

00:02:40.377 --> 00:02:44.240
The plant is exclusively for
your office and the office

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.140
is exclusively business,
I'll give you the plant.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.140
A new laptop computer.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.370
If it's exclusively used for business,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.720
then 100% would be used for business.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.380
If you don't have a personal computer,

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.160
the IRS would say keep your time,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.470
deduct that percentage of the laptop.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.510
How about children's books?

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:04.907
I have to give you a nay on that one.

00:03:04.907 --> 00:03:07.110
How about books that advance my career?

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.340
Continuing education,
whether it's a seminar,

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.420
a book would be deductible.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:13.750
And what about meals?

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.440
So meals are deductible
if business is conducted.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.840
Meals are 50% deductible.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.820
There is a change I
wanna mention for 2021.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.730
Meals are gonna be 100% deductible.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.360
That helps to stimulate the economy

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.440
and get us all out going there.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.010
When should people file their taxes?

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.830
If you're able and you have the forms,

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.310
the earlier the better to file.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.490
There's a lot of people
stealing people's identity

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.920
and you wanna claim your identity first.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.063
Easiest way, file early.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.570
How do charitable donations
affect your taxes?

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.913
In years past, unless you
itemized deductions, not much.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.150
Yeah, so this is actually a change

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.120
that Congress has talked
about for a long time

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.800
and was finally able to get done

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.540
in the CARES Act, which passed last March.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.970
We wanna demonstrate that we do care

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.250
for the American people in every way.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.150
What Congress said is,

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.770
look, even if you take
the standard deduction,

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:15.830
you can get a tax break of up to $300

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:16.900
for charitable donations.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:20.140
So typically, that was only
limited to people who itemized.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:21.500
So it was a big deal.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:24.090
Congress' thinking here
is this was a way for them

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.420
to get more money out to groups

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.850
that were helping people
during the pandemic

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.550
without specifically sending
them a check directly.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:33.010
And whenever you donate to charity,

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.070
you're going to wanna make sure

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.200
that that organization is
putting your money to good use.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.760
But how do you do that?

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.150
Charity Navigator is the nation's

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:41.983
and actually for that matter,

00:04:41.983 --> 00:04:45.400
the world's largest independent
evaluator of nonprofits.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.840
We get to help donors
find strong organizations

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.560
to make donations to.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.120
You wanna know you're giving to a 501

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.490
legitimate nonprofit organization.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:57.850
And that's where we
really can be very helpful

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.840
because our database has the full list

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.580
of legally registered organizations.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.340
If you don't find it, it may not be real.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.903
And that's an important
thing to know about.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.140
Getting audited, now,
that's a scary prospect.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:15.660
And one strange or slightly
infuriating aspect of it

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.060
is that those who make
very little get audited

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.590
just about as much as
those who make a lot.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.560
It's true, an investigation
by ProPublica last year

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.490
revealed that over the last 10 years,

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.130
audits for those claiming
the earned income tax credit,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.160
the nation's largest anti-poverty
program, remains steady.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:33.230
While millionaires were 80% less likely

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:35.380
to be audited than they were in 2011.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:36.810
All of this might lead you to believe

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.450
that if you're ultra wealthy,

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.803
the system works a bit
differently for you.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:46.050
I'm Ben Steverman, I
cover wealth at Bloomberg.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:48.580
Okay, Ben, let's say I'm a newly-minted

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.030
ultra wealthy person.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.270
How do I make sure that
Uncle Sam doesn't take

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.530
even a substantial portion of my income?

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:58.190
If you have a bunch of
wealth, you have an advantage

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:03.010
over people like me or you who
every time we get a paycheck,

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.380
the IRS knows about it

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.410
and they're gonna expect
their cut of that.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:09.190
We don't have a control over
when we can take income,

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:10.430
but wealthy people do.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:13.350
And I think that's the
key to the advantage

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:16.860
that wealthy people have on
avoiding taxes on their income.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.720
We have situations where
people have billions of dollars

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.870
but are only reporting maybe
10, $20 million in income

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:24.930
in a given year.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.810
Wait, how is that possible?

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:29.550
The best example is probably Jeff Bezos.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.810
His wealth comes from Amazon.com

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:36.350
and he's worth almost $200 billion.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:40.190
However, he hasn't paid taxes
yet on a good portion of that

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:42.900
because all that wealth
is just in the share price

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.840
of Amazon going up and
he hasn't sold that many

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:46.930
of those shares.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.220
The thing is that if he
never sells those shares

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.950
or if he can somehow
transfer them to his kids,

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:55.070
he might never have to
pay taxes on that money.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:58.270
That's a loophole that is in the law.

00:06:58.270 --> 00:06:59.460
Joe Biden has actually said

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.870
that he wants to change that rule.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:04.120
The rule is called stepped-up basis.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:06.810
And this idea that when
your kids or whoever

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:11.150
inherits your money, all those
gains that would be taxable

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.280
are wiped out and they can start fresh.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.310
And they never have to pay taxes on that.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.410
But if his fortune is all in stock shares,

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.000
how can he afford to buy anything?

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.960
So you can borrow against shares,

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:25.880
you can borrow against buildings you own.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:27.760
That's something that Donald Trump

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:29.730
and other real estate developers do.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.400
You can use that debt to
maybe finance your lifestyle.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.250
And you're probably gonna
be okay as long as the value

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:38.160
of those assets you're borrowing
against continues to rise

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:41.370
and then you can continue
to refinance that money.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.810
Geez, so I guess the lesson
here is to stop working a job

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:46.850
and become a high net
worth shareholder then.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:51.850
Yes, investors pay much
lower rates than workers do,

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.630
even if we're talking
about the working rich.

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:58.570
The working rich can end
up paying a top tax rate

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:02.700
of about 50% if you're looking
at New York or California,

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.580
which have the top income tax rates.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:07.300
But these are people
like doctors and actors

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:08.850
and entertainers and athletes

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:10.250
that end up paying this rate.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:13.920
It's not investors because
capital gains and dividends

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.050
are taxed at a much lower rate.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.720
Donald Trump paying 750
or almost nothing in taxes

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.530
is maybe an extreme example of something

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.690
that business owners do all the time.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.810
Because you control these
businesses, you're able

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:30.610
to generate losses that maybe
are just losses on paper

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:33.200
but they can help offset other income.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:35.380
One of the billionaire's
favorite things to do is

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:36.490
to buy a sports team.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:40.040
Sports teams are wonderful
for creating paper losses.

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:42.510
There's this idea of depreciation.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.320
Let's say you build a factory.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.050
The value of that factory is
gonna lose value over time

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:51.060
just naturally and the IRS
says, okay, well, you can deduct

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.790
that loss of value of wear
and tear and everything

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:54.990
on your taxes.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:58.860
Well, sports teams, real estate
buildings have the same rule

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:01.530
and you're able to
depreciate those over time,

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:03.763
even if the actual value of those things

00:09:03.763 --> 00:09:06.350
like the sports team for
example is actually going up.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:09.370
The wealthy can hire very
expensive advisors who can come up

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:13.290
with these schemes to lower
their tax burden or avoid

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:16.350
not just the income tax
but the estate tax as well.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:19.210
So the other thing that's
happened is that the IRS budget

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:22.320
has just been slashed over
the last several years.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.720
You actually have about
40% fewer agents employed

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.650
by the IRS than were employed in 2010.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.770
So you have a situation where
nobody's really checking

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:35.200
a lot of this stuff.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:39.530
My W2, your W2, your paycheck,
the IRS has computer systems

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:41.530
that can quickly analyze
that and figure out

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:43.140
that, oh, you didn't pay taxes.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:47.010
But if I set up some
complex trust strategy

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:50.270
using offshore vehicles
and then I'm also taking

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:52.720
all this depreciation off of my business

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.960
and reported all these
losses off of my business

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.320
and oh, by the way, I'm
employing some family members

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.760
at that business, there
are so many different ways

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:03.120
that a business owner and an investor

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.470
can just structure things
in very complex ways

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:08.950
that the IRS just really
doesn't have the resources

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:10.380
to even take a look.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:11.770
And that's why people are saying we need

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:14.950
to spend a lot more money on
the IRS and that if we did so,

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:18.070
we could actually find
trillions of dollars possibly

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:20.130
in new revenue just from people

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:21.500
who aren't paying their taxes

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:25.143
or who are using some questionable
schemes to avoid taxes.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:29.190
So if you can't get that
tricky with your income taxes,

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:32.200
just remember that stimulus
payments aren't taxed,

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:35.520
unemployment payments are
and this one's gonna hurt,

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:38.163
but file as early as possible.

